00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:16.440
Imagine you placed a market order for an energy ETF or an oil services name in the first few minutes

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.500
after the Hormuz blockade news broke. Your fill came 42 cents worse than the last quoted price you saw

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:28.420
on the screen. That happened because the bid-ask spread blew out dramatically from roughly 8 cents to

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:34.480
51 cents within the first 12 minutes of trading. Market makers widened spreads to protect against

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.860
inventory risk while high-frequency algorithms temporarily pulled quotes. On average, spreads in oil

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.920
service and exploration stocks can expand 400 to 600 percent during geopolitical spikes.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:53.720
On a $5,000 position that seemingly flat day actually delivered an invisible 0.8 to 1.2 percent slippage

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:59.620
hit before you even started. The pro tip most retail investors still miss is to always check the spread

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:05.120
to price ratio and the 20-day average daily volume before you ever hit the market order button.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.640
If that ratio exceeds 0.25%, immediately switch to limit orders placed inside the current spread

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.980
or when available on your platform route part of the order through dark pools.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:23.340
Canadian TFSA traders face an extra layer of complexity. Frequent trading and low volume names can

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:28.640
sometimes trigger CRA scrutiny under business income rules, so documenting your process and keeping

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:34.140
position sizes reasonable is prudent. The biggest misconception is assuming the displayed last trade price

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:39.740
is exactly what you will actually pay. Instead, always verify real-time bid-ask depth on your

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:44.860
platform and size every order to no more than 5 percent of the 30-day average daily volume.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:50.080
Doing so helps you avoid adverse selection and keeps more of your capital compounding instead of

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:56.020
leaking away in invisible friction. These are the small but consistent edges that separate investors

00:10:56.020 --> 00:11:00.100
who steadily compound from those who wonder why their returns lagged the benchmarks.

